    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d662">

                <pb facs="1355" n="1321" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v08/pg1321"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">768.5/12–1852</note>
                <head>No. 662<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Director of the Joint
                        Staff of the Joint Chiefs of Staff (Cabell)
                        to the Secretary of Defense (Lovett)</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d662-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-12-12">12 December 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Report of Tripartite Exploratory Military Discussions with
                        Yugoslavia</item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>General Handy, Deputy United
                        States Commander in Chief, Europe, acting as representative of Great
                        Britain, France and the United States, recently conducted military
                        discussions with the Yugoslav Government. . . . A report of General
                            Handy’s discussions has been
                        received and is now under study.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>As one of his recommendations, General Handy has urged that the Tripartite Powers communicate in
                        the near future to Yugoslavia: <list>
                            <label>a.</label>
                            <item>Their gratification that the initial exploratory military
                                conversations have taken place;</item>
                            <label>b.</label>
                            <item>That Greneral Handy’s
                                report of those conversations is under study; and</item>
                            <label>c.</label>
                            <item>That from the report, they note that General Handy left with the Yugoslavs
                                certain general suggestions as to how to proceed toward further
                                planning and intimated that Yugoslav counter-suggestions on this
                                point would be welcomed.</item>
                        </list></item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The Joint Chiefs of Staff consider this proposed action sound. Accordingly
                        they recommend that you notify the Secretary of State of their views on this
                        matter and that you recommend that the United States, in coordination with
                        France and the United Kingdom, <pb facs="1356" n="1322" xml:id="pg1322"/>
                        make the proposed approach to the Yugoslav Government at an early
                        date.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>For the Joint Chiefs of Staff:<lb/><signed><hi
                                rendition="#small-caps">C.P. Cabell</hi><lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Lieut. General, USAF</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d662-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d662-f1-content">
                <p>Transmitted to the Secretary of State as an attachment to a letter of Dec. 18
                    from Acting Secretary of Defense William C.
                        Foster, in which Foster
                    noted that he concurred in the recommendation it contained.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
